  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{3x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{3x+2}$ on the interval $x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{3x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}=1\\,\u27f9\\,k=\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\n$$",
